1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,713
Go back to the very first thing this course showed you: Dana and Sam, independently,

2
00:00:04,713 --> 00:00:08,453
each picking their favourite from four variants, and both of them wrong.

3
00:00:08,453 --> 00:00:10,843
That was never really an argument about taste.

4
00:00:10,843 --> 00:00:12,765
It was an argument about instruments.

5
00:00:12,765 --> 00:00:14,687
Taste has no way to measure a winner.

6
00:00:14,687 --> 00:00:16,817
An ad account, structured properly, does.

7
00:00:16,817 --> 00:00:20,453
Sit with that reframing, because it changes what you're building here.

8
00:00:20,453 --> 00:00:22,790
You're not setting up a place to spend money.

9
00:00:22,790 --> 00:00:27,154
You're building the one instrument in this course that can answer the question every

10
00:00:27,154 --> 00:00:31,517
other lecture assumed you'd eventually need: which of these angles is actually true.

11
00:00:31,517 --> 00:00:35,673
Most accounts aren't structured properly, and it's rarely because someone made a

12
00:00:35,673 --> 00:00:36,556
careless mistake.

13
00:00:36,556 --> 00:00:40,815
It's because the default way to build a campaign, one budget, one audience, a pile

14
00:00:40,815 --> 00:00:45,178
of creative thrown in together, optimises for spending efficiently, not for learning

15
00:00:45,178 --> 00:00:45,646
anything.

16
00:00:45,646 --> 00:00:49,853
Those are different goals, and this section is about building for the second one.

17
00:00:49,853 --> 00:00:54,009
Ask yourself honestly which goal your own account has actually been built for so

18
00:00:54,009 --> 00:00:54,217
far.

19
00:00:54,217 --> 00:00:58,528
If every campaign you've run has been optimised purely to spend efficiently, that's

20
00:00:58,528 --> 00:00:59,775
not a failure of effort.

21
00:00:59,775 --> 00:01:04,034
It's the default every platform nudges you toward, because efficient spend is what

22
00:01:04,034 --> 00:01:05,852
the platform gets rewarded for too.

23
00:01:05,852 --> 00:01:09,437
Nobody's default setting is on your side of this particular question.

24
00:01:09,987 --> 00:01:12,972
Here's the entire structural idea, in one comparison.

25
00:01:12,972 --> 00:01:17,703
Throw five different angles into one ad set and the delivery system optimises within

26
00:01:17,703 --> 00:01:22,153
that set for whatever gets early traction, which tells you almost nothing about

27
00:01:22,153 --> 00:01:24,068
which angle was actually stronger.

28
00:01:24,068 --> 00:01:28,405
It's rewarding speed of early signal, not the thing you're trying to measure.

29
00:01:28,405 --> 00:01:33,080
Give each angle its own ad set instead, same budget, same audience, same placement,

30
00:01:33,080 --> 00:01:37,023
and the only thing left different between them is the creative itself.

31
00:01:37,023 --> 00:01:39,276
That's not a minor configuration choice.

32
00:01:39,276 --> 00:01:43,782
It's the difference between an account that produces an answer and one that just

33
00:01:43,782 --> 00:01:45,133
produces a spend report.

34
00:01:45,133 --> 00:01:49,808
This is the same logic a real scientific experiment runs on, just applied to a Meta

35
00:01:49,808 --> 00:01:52,005
or TikTok ads manager instead of a lab.

36
00:01:52,005 --> 00:01:56,736
Change one variable, hold everything else constant, and whatever difference shows up

37
00:01:56,736 --> 00:02:00,172
in the result can only be explained by the thing you changed.

38
00:02:00,172 --> 00:02:04,791
Change five variables at once, mixed into one set, and you've got a result with no

39
00:02:04,791 --> 00:02:06,593
way to trace it back to a cause.

40
00:02:07,143 --> 00:02:11,446
Here's what that looks like on Harlan's own account, structurally, before any

41
00:02:11,446 --> 00:02:14,183
specific budget numbers, those come next lecture.

42
00:02:14,183 --> 00:02:18,653
One campaign, one ad set per angle, everything held constant except the creative

43
00:02:18,653 --> 00:02:19,044
itself.

44
00:02:19,044 --> 00:02:23,570
Notice what this buys you that a single mixed ad set never could: when one ad set

45
00:02:23,570 --> 00:02:28,208
clearly outperforms the others, you know, with real confidence, that the difference

46
00:02:28,208 --> 00:02:28,934
is the angle.

47
00:02:28,934 --> 00:02:33,292
Not the audience, not the placement, not which ad happened to get shown first.

48
00:02:33,292 --> 00:02:37,985
The angle, isolated, the same way a fair experiment isolates one variable at a time.

49
00:02:37,985 --> 00:02:42,176
This is also why the angle map from Section 2.4 mattered as much as it did.

50
00:02:42,176 --> 00:02:45,528
A structure this clean is only as good as what goes into it.

51
00:02:45,528 --> 00:02:49,663
Feed it twenty genuinely distinct angles and you learn twenty real things.

52
00:02:49,663 --> 00:02:54,077
Feed it twenty near-identical variations on one idea and you've built a perfect

53
00:02:54,077 --> 00:02:57,597
instrument pointed at a question too narrow to be worth asking.

54
00:02:57,597 --> 00:03:02,011
That's a genuinely easy mistake to make under pressure to hit a creative volume

55
00:03:02,011 --> 00:03:02,402
target.

56
00:03:02,402 --> 00:03:06,257
Twenty ad sets sounds thorough regardless of what's actually in them.

57
00:03:06,257 --> 00:03:10,671
Only the person who built the angle map honestly knows whether those twenty are

58
00:03:10,671 --> 00:03:14,694
twenty real bets or one bet wearing nineteen slightly different outfits.

59
00:03:15,244 --> 00:03:19,086
Notice the trap in the loose version: it doesn't look like a mistake.

60
00:03:19,086 --> 00:03:23,540
"Let the algorithm figure it out" sounds sophisticated, like trusting the system

61
00:03:23,540 --> 00:03:25,099
instead of micromanaging it.

62
00:03:25,099 --> 00:03:29,665
But the algorithm is optimising for its own objective, spend efficiency inside the

63
00:03:29,665 --> 00:03:34,230
set you gave it, not for answering your question about which angle actually works.

64
00:03:34,230 --> 00:03:36,290
That's not the algorithm failing you.

65
00:03:36,290 --> 00:03:38,573
It's doing exactly what it's built to do.

66
00:03:38,573 --> 00:03:43,194
The mistake is asking it a question it was never structured to answer, then reading

67
00:03:43,194 --> 00:03:44,530
its output as if it had.

68
00:03:44,530 --> 00:03:48,372
Why does this trap catch experienced marketers as often as beginners?

69
00:03:48,372 --> 00:03:52,715
Because "trust the algorithm" is genuinely good advice in other contexts, like

70
00:03:52,715 --> 00:03:57,225
letting Advantage+ find the best audience inside a targeting range you've already

71
00:03:57,225 --> 00:03:57,448
set.

72
00:03:57,448 --> 00:03:59,563
The mistake isn't trusting automation.

73
00:03:59,563 --> 00:04:03,906
It's trusting it to answer a question, which angle actually works, that only a

74
00:04:03,906 --> 00:04:07,136
properly isolated structure can answer in the first place.

75
00:04:07,136 --> 00:04:11,590
Worth naming: this is the same discipline Section 4.6's workshop asked of you by

76
00:04:11,590 --> 00:04:14,207
insisting on three finished ads instead of one.

77
00:04:14,207 --> 00:04:17,993
A single ad, however good, tells you nothing about which angle wins.

78
00:04:17,993 --> 00:04:22,447
Both mistakes come from the same instinct, wanting an answer faster than a clean

79
00:04:22,447 --> 00:04:24,117
test can actually deliver one.

80
00:04:24,667 --> 00:04:28,581
One honest trade-off worth naming, because a tidy structure isn't free.

81
00:04:28,581 --> 00:04:32,991
Splitting budget across several isolated ad sets means each one gets less spend,

82
00:04:32,991 --> 00:04:36,354
less delivery efficiency, than one larger combined set would.

83
00:04:36,354 --> 00:04:37,401
That's a real cost.

84
00:04:37,401 --> 00:04:41,701
It's also the price of an actual answer instead of a guess dressed up as data.

85
00:04:41,701 --> 00:04:45,945
Section 1.3 already made this argument in the abstract, that your taste can't

86
00:04:45,945 --> 00:04:47,213
reliably pick a winner.

87
00:04:47,213 --> 00:04:51,623
This structure is where that argument becomes something you can actually act on.

88
00:04:51,623 --> 00:04:54,434
Weigh that cost honestly rather than dismissing it.

89
00:04:54,434 --> 00:04:58,679
A slightly less efficient test that tells you the truth beats a slightly more

90
00:04:58,679 --> 00:05:01,490
efficient one that tells you nothing you can trust.

91
00:05:01,490 --> 00:05:04,412
You're not choosing between cheap and expensive here.

92
00:05:04,412 --> 00:05:08,987
You're choosing between an answer and a guess wearing a spend report as a disguise.

93
00:05:08,987 --> 00:05:13,507
There's also an angle-map cost worth naming honestly, the one this lecture's table

94
00:05:13,507 --> 00:05:14,830
slide already hinted at.

95
00:05:14,830 --> 00:05:18,137
A structure this clean is only as good as what goes into it.

96
00:05:18,137 --> 00:05:22,602
Twenty isolated ad sets running twenty near-identical variations on one idea will

97
00:05:22,602 --> 00:05:27,012
still produce a clean result, just a clean answer to a question too narrow to be

98
00:05:27,012 --> 00:05:28,997
worth the structure built to ask it.

99
00:05:29,547 --> 00:05:34,307
Next lecture puts real numbers on this structure: how much to spend per ad set, and

100
00:05:34,307 --> 00:05:39,067
the exact rule that tells you when to kill one, decided now, before a single result

101
00:05:39,067 --> 00:05:41,819
comes in to tempt you into moving the goalposts.

102
00:05:41,819 --> 00:05:46,465
Before that, look at whatever campaign you're currently running, if you have one,

103
00:05:46,465 --> 00:05:50,823
and ask the one question this lecture actually turns on: if I saw one ad set

104
00:05:50,823 --> 00:05:54,952
outperform the others tomorrow, could I say with confidence exactly why?

105
00:05:54,952 --> 00:05:57,877
If the honest answer is no, that's not a small gap.

106
00:05:57,877 --> 00:06:00,687
It's the entire gap this section exists to close.
